---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import AdSlot from "../components/AdSlot.astro";
import { ISSUES, VIGEM } from "../consts";

const faq = [
  {
    q: "My PC does not appear in the app",
    a: "Almost always one of three things: the companion is not running, the two devices are on different networks, or Windows Firewall is blocking it.",
  },
  {
    q: "Windows sees the pad but the game ignores it",
    a: "The game was started before the controller existed. Hold the XBOX button on the phone for a second to re-attach the pad, which produces the arrival event the game was waiting for.",
  },
  {
    q: "The pairing code is not accepted",
    a: "The pairing window is open for 120 seconds and closes on the first successful pairing. Click 'Pair a phone' on the PC to open a fresh one.",
  },
  {
    q: "The controls feel laggy",
    a: "Check the round trip on the phone's Diagnostics screen. Under 10 ms is normal. Higher usually means a congested 2.4 GHz network or a phone that has fallen back to a distant access point.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: f.a },
  })),
};
---

<Base
  title="Troubleshooting"
  description="Fixes for the things that actually go wrong with PhonePad: the PC not showing up, a game ignoring the controller, pairing failing, and input feeling laggy."
  article
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} slot="head" />

  <section>
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h1 style="font-size:clamp(2rem,5vw,3rem)">When it does not work</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        In roughly the order things actually go wrong.
      </p>

      <h2>My PC does not appear in the app</h2>
      <p>Three things, in the order worth checking:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Is the companion running on the PC?</strong> It has to be
          open. It is not a service and does not start with Windows.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Are both devices on the same Wi-Fi?</strong> Not the same
          building — the same network. A phone on a 5 GHz guest network and a PC
          on a 2.4 GHz main network cannot see each other, and neither can a
          phone that has quietly fallen back to mobile data.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Is the firewall blocking it?</strong> Open
          <em>Advanced</em> → <em>Network</em> in the companion. If the firewall
          rule is missing, click <em>Add firewall rule</em> and accept the
          elevation prompt.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Still nothing? Some routers have <strong>AP isolation</strong> or
        "client isolation" turned on, which stops devices on the network from
        talking to each other at all. It is often on by default on guest
        networks. Turn it off, or use your main network.
      </p>

      <h2>Windows sees the pad but the game ignores it</h2>
      <p>
        The single most common report, and the most confusing, because the
        controller demonstrably works — the Xbox app's own menus respond, the
        Guide button opens the overlay, <code>joy.cpl</code> shows every axis
        moving. Only the game ignores you.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Hold the XBOX button on the phone for a second.</strong>
      </p>
      <p>
        A cloud gaming client binds a controller to a streaming session at the
        moment it observes one <em>arrive</em>. A pad that already existed when
        the stream started never produces an arrival event, so the session ends
        up with no controller bound. Holding XBOX unplugs the virtual pad and
        plugs it back in, which produces a real arrival event without disturbing
        your connection.
      </p>
      <p>
        The companion can do this for you — tick <em>"Do this automatically when
        a cloud game starts"</em>.
      </p>

      <AdSlot />

      <h2>"No controller driver"</h2>
      <p>
        ViGEmBus is not installed, or the install did not finish.
        <a href={VIGEM} rel="noopener">Install ViGEmBus 1.22.0</a>, then restart
        the companion. If it still fails, reboot — the driver has to load, and
        occasionally an upgrade over an old version needs one.
      </p>

      <h2>The pairing code is not accepted</h2>
      <p>
        The pairing window is open for <strong>120 seconds</strong> and closes
        the moment a phone pairs successfully. If it has expired, click
        <em>Pair a phone</em> on the PC for a fresh code.
      </p>
      <p>
        If the phone says the PC is not in pairing mode, that is the PC
        answering honestly rather than a stale screen — open the window and try
        again.
      </p>

      <h2>The controls feel laggy</h2>
      <p>
        Open <em>Diagnostics</em> on the phone and look at the round trip.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Under 10 ms</strong> — normal, and not your problem.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>10–30 ms</strong> — usually a busy 2.4 GHz network. Move to 5
          GHz if you can.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Above 30 ms, or spiky</strong> — the phone may have roamed to
          a distant access point, or something is saturating the network.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If <em>loss</em> is above zero, that is the network, not PhonePad. The
        protocol re-sends unconditionally, so a few dropped packets are
        invisible, but sustained loss is worth chasing.
      </p>

      <h2>Everything drops when a second finger lands</h2>
      <p>
        Look at the connection status. If it is flickering between connected and
        reconnecting, the network is dropping packets and the symptom is the
        link, not the touchscreen. Fix the link.
      </p>

      <h2>A stick drifts, or does not centre</h2>
      <p>
        Rest a thumb still on the stick and watch the axis in Diagnostics. If it
        flutters, raise <em>Deadzone</em> for that control in the layout editor
        — it is set to reject digitiser noise, and some screens are noisier than
        others.
      </p>

      <h2>Steering is twitchy, or numb</h2>
      <p>
        PhonePad starts its output just past the deadzone the game applies, and
        games differ in how large that is. If a game feels twitchy, lower
        <em>Anti-deadzone</em> for that stick; if it feels numb near centre,
        raise it. The layout editor has both, per control.
      </p>

      <h2>None of this helped</h2>
      <p>
        <a href={ISSUES} rel="noopener">Open an issue</a>. Include what the
        Diagnostics screen says and what the companion's Advanced section shows,
        especially the rejection breakdown if anything is being rejected — that
        line is usually the whole diagnosis.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
